  "findings": [
    "CHORDWISE resolution carries the error: 88.3 % and 88.5 % recovered at alpha 0 and 4, identical, so it is not an incidence effect.",
    "Spanwise gives 11-12 %, edge spacing 7 %. Neither is where the error lives.",
    "Wall-normal refinement at FIXED first-cell height moves CDp the WRONG way, -15.4 % at both angles, while lowering CDv 2.7-2.9 %. More layers at the same s0 resolves the outer boundary layer differently; it is not a route to converged pressure drag. First-cell height itself remains untested.",
    "The four directions are close to additive -- chord + span - normal + edges accounts for roughly nine tenths of what global refinement does -- so the decomposition is essentially complete.",
    "Physically consistent with CDv being converged while CDp is not: pressure drag on a swept wing is set by how well the chordwise pressure distribution is resolved, from leading-edge suction through trailing-edge recovery."
